WEEK 4: Predictive Modeling and Optimization for Logistics Delivery Performance

1. Executive Summary
This report presents the fourth and final stage of the logistics analytics project, focusing on predictive modeling and optimization. The objective was to predict actual shipping time and use the predictions to identify potential schedule gaps and operational risks.
The DataCo SMART SUPPLY CHAIN FOR BIG DATA ANALYSIS dataset was used for this project. After data preparation, machine learning regression models were developed to predict the target variable, Days for shipping (real).
Three models were evaluated: Linear Regression, Decision Tree Regressor, and Random Forest Regressor. Model performance was measured using Mean Absolute Error (MAE), Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), and R². Random Forest produced the best overall baseline performance, achieving an MAE of 0.9851 days, RMSE of 1.2651 days, and R² of 0.3928.
Five-fold cross-validation also showed that Random Forest had the highest mean R² of 0.3923. Hyperparameter tuning slightly improved MAE but did not improve RMSE or R², so the original Random Forest model was retained as the final model.
The final analysis was extended into an optimization framework. Model predictions were compared with scheduled shipping times to identify potential delay risks. The results were used to propose dynamic scheduling, risk-based shipment prioritization, regional resource allocation, shipping-mode review, route-planning improvements, and targeted cost-minimization strategies.

2. Introduction
Logistics organizations need accurate delivery-time estimates to manage inventory, transportation capacity, warehouse operations, staffing, customer commitments, and shipment priorities.
Unexpected delivery delays can increase operational costs and negatively affect customer satisfaction. Predictive analytics can help logistics teams estimate shipping duration before delivery and identify shipments that may exceed their scheduled delivery time.
The previous project stages focused on strategic planning, data cleaning, preprocessing, exploratory analysis, and visualization. Week 4 extends this work by applying supervised machine learning and using model predictions to support logistics optimization.
The overall workflow is:
Data Preparation → Feature Engineering → Model Training → Model Evaluation → Prediction → Delay Analysis → Optimization Recommendations
3. Problem Definition
The logistics prediction problem was defined as a regression problem.
Objective
Predict the actual number of days required for a shipment to be delivered.
Target Variable
Days for shipping (real)
The predicted shipping time is then compared with:
Days for shipment (scheduled)
to calculate the predicted schedule deviation.
Schedule Deviation
The following formula was used:
Schedule Deviation = Predicted Shipping Days − Scheduled Shipping Days
If the predicted shipping time is greater than the scheduled time, the shipment is considered potentially at risk of delay.

4. Dataset Description
The project uses the DataCo SMART SUPPLY CHAIN FOR BIG DATA ANALYSIS dataset.
The dataset contains information related to orders, customers, products, sales, profits, shipping, and delivery performance.
Dataset Summary
Item	Value
Total records	180,519
Original columns	56
Processed columns	58
Unique orders	65,752
Missing values after preprocessing	0
Duplicate rows	0
The dataset was cleaned and prepared during Week 2 before being used for predictive modeling.

