6. Data Preparation
A preprocessing pipeline was developed using Scikit-learn.
Numerical features were handled using median imputation.
Categorical features were handled using most-frequent imputation followed by one-hot encoding.
The dataset was divided into:
•	80% training data
•	20% testing data
The train-test split used random_state=42 to ensure reproducibility.
Python Code
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split

X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(
    X,
    y,
    test_size=0.20,
    random_state=42
)
Preprocessing Pipeline
from sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer
from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline
from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder
from sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer

numerical_transformer = Pipeline(
    steps=[
        ("imputer", SimpleImputer(strategy="median"))
    ]
)

categorical_transformer = Pipeline(
    steps=[
        ("imputer", SimpleImputer(strategy="most_frequent")),
        ("onehot", OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown="ignore"))
    ]
)

preprocessor = ColumnTransformer(
    transformers=[
        ("num", numerical_transformer, numerical_features),
        ("cat", categorical_transformer, categorical_features)
    ]
)

7. Model Selection
Three regression models were selected for comparison.
7.1 Linear Regression
Linear Regression was used as a simple baseline model. It provides an interpretable benchmark for determining whether more complex models provide meaningful improvement.
7.2 Decision Tree Regressor
Decision Tree Regression was selected because it can capture nonlinear relationships and interactions between features.
7.3 Random Forest Regressor
Random Forest was selected because it combines multiple decision trees and can capture nonlinear relationships more effectively than a single decision tree.
It also provides feature-importance information, which is useful for business interpretation.

8. Model Training
The following models were trained:
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression
from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeRegressor
from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor

linear_model = LinearRegression()

decision_tree = DecisionTreeRegressor(
    max_depth=10,
    random_state=42
)

random_forest = RandomForestRegressor(
    n_estimators=100,
    max_depth=15,
    random_state=42,
    n_jobs=-1
)
Each model was integrated with the preprocessing pipeline.

9. Model Performance Evaluation
The following metrics were used:
Mean Absolute Error (MAE)
MAE measures the average absolute difference between actual and predicted values.
Lower MAE indicates better performance.
Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)
RMSE gives greater importance to larger prediction errors.
Lower RMSE indicates better performance.
R² Score
R² measures how much variation in the target variable is explained by the model.
Higher R² indicates better performance.

10. Baseline Model Results
Model	MAE	RMSE	R²
Decision Tree	0.9847	1.2697	0.3884
Random Forest	0.9851	1.2651	0.3928
Linear Regression	0.9860	1.2662	0.3918
Interpretation
Random Forest achieved:
•	Lowest RMSE: 1.2651
•	Highest R²: 0.3928
Decision Tree achieved a slightly lower MAE of 0.9847, but Random Forest performed better on RMSE and R².
Therefore, Random Forest was selected as the best overall baseline model.

11. Cross-Validation
Five-fold cross-validation was performed to determine whether the models provided stable performance across different subsets of the training data.
Model	Mean CV R²	Standard Deviation
Linear Regression	0.391694	0.004377
Decision Tree	0.387756	0.004296
Random Forest	0.392329	0.004598
Interpretation
Random Forest achieved the highest mean CV R² of 0.392329.
The small standard deviation indicates that model performance was relatively stable across the five folds.
The results also show that Linear Regression performed very close to Random Forest. This suggests that the current feature set provides moderate predictive information and that additional operational features could improve future models.

12. Hyperparameter Tuning
Hyperparameter tuning was performed using RandomizedSearchCV.
A smaller search space was used to reduce computational cost.
fast_param_grid = {
    "model__n_estimators": [50, 75, 100],
    "model__max_depth": [10, 15],
    "model__min_samples_split": [2, 5],
    "model__min_samples_leaf": [1, 2]
}
The selected parameters were:
Parameter	Best Value
n_estimators	100
max_depth	10
min_samples_split	2
min_samples_leaf	2
Tuned Model Results
•	MAE: 0.9826
•	RMSE: 1.2653
•	R²: 0.3926

13. Original vs Tuned Random Forest
Model	MAE	RMSE	R²
Original Random Forest	0.9851	1.2651	0.3928
Tuned Random Forest	0.9826	1.2653	0.3926
The tuned model slightly improved MAE.
However, the original Random Forest achieved:
•	Lower RMSE
•	Higher R²
Therefore, the original Random Forest was retained as the final model.
This is important because hyperparameter tuning does not automatically mean that a model has improved overall performance.

14. Prediction Error Analysis
Prediction results were compared with actual shipping time.
The prediction error was calculated as:
prediction_results = pd.DataFrame({
    "Actual": y_test.values,
    "Predicted": y_pred_tuned
})

prediction_results["Error"] = (
    prediction_results["Actual"]
    - prediction_results["Predicted"]
)

prediction_results["Absolute_Error"] = (
    prediction_results["Error"].abs()
)
For the tuned model, the absolute error statistics were:
Statistic	Value
Mean	0.9826
Standard Deviation	0.7972
Median	0.9945
Maximum	2.6760
The results indicate that the model provides useful estimates for broad operational planning, although prediction errors remain significant.
 

15. Feature Importance
Feature importance was analyzed using the Random Forest model.
Feature	Importance
Days for shipment (scheduled)	92.3718%
Shipping Mode – Same Day	2.9713%
Shipping Mode – First Class	2.1848%
Order Profit Per Order	0.6830%
Order Item Total	0.4312%
Order Item Discount	0.3765%
Order Item Product Price	0.1453%
Order Item Quantity	0.0610%
Interpretation
Days for shipment (scheduled) was by far the most important predictive feature, with approximately 92.37% feature importance.
This indicates that scheduled shipping duration provides the strongest predictive contribution within the current model.
However, feature importance indicates predictive contribution and does not prove causation.
 

16. Optimization Method
The trained model was used to estimate potential schedule deviations.
Three additional measures were calculated:
Predicted Schedule Deviation
Predicted Shipping Days − Scheduled Shipping Days
Predicted Delay Days
max(Predicted Schedule Deviation, 0)
Predicted Delay Risk
A shipment was classified as potentially delayed when:
Predicted Schedule Deviation > 0
This approach allows logistics managers to prioritize shipments that may require intervention.

17. Overall Optimization Results
The model was evaluated on the 36,104-record test set.
Metric	Result
Test shipments	36,104
Predicted delay shipments	22,382
Predicted delay rate	61.99%
Average predicted shipping time	3.4984 days
Average scheduled shipping time	2.9339 days
Average predicted delay	0.5739 days
Interpretation
The average predicted shipping time was approximately 3.50 days, while the average scheduled shipping time was approximately 2.93 days.
This gives an average predicted schedule gap of approximately 0.57 days.
The 61.99% figure represents a model-based predicted delay rate on the test data. It should not be interpreted as the historical actual delay rate.
 

18. Shipping-Mode Optimization
Shipping Mode	Shipments	Avg Predicted Shipping	Avg Scheduled	Avg Predicted Delay
First Class	5,531	2.0000	1.0	1.0000
Same Day	1,961	0.4782	0.0	0.4782
Second Class	7,026	3.9966	2.0	1.9966
Standard Class	21,586	3.9946	4.0	0.0103
Key Finding
Second Class has the largest predicted schedule gap, approximately 2 days.
This indicates that Second Class shipments should receive additional schedule review and operational monitoring.
The Same Day category requires careful interpretation because the dataset records the scheduled shipping value as zero. Therefore, a 100% predicted gap rate for Same Day should not be interpreted as evidence that Same Day delivery is operationally 100% late.
Standard Class has a very small average predicted schedule deviation.
 

19. Regional Optimization
The model-based regional analysis identified several areas with relatively high predicted delay risk.
Region	Shipments	Avg Predicted Days	Avg Scheduled Days	Predicted Delay Rate
Central Asia	108	3.3403	2.7500	86.11%
Central Africa	360	3.5748	2.9361	84.44%
East of USA	1,385	3.4907	2.9061	81.52%
West Africa	712	3.5297	2.9846	77.81%
East Africa	374	3.5017	2.9332	70.59%
Western Europe	5,493	3.5019	2.9099	70.20%
Southern Africa	213	3.3175	2.7700	67.61%
North Africa	641	3.4432	2.8861	63.65%
South Asia	1,556	3.5047	2.9030	62.34%
US Center	1,202	3.4992	2.9085	60.73%
Interpretation
Central Asia, Central Africa, and East of USA show relatively high model-based predicted delay rates.
These regions should be considered for additional operational investigation and resource planning.
However, the results do not prove that geography itself causes delays. Additional factors such as distance, carrier, traffic, weather, and route complexity would be required for causal analysis.
 

20. Optimization Recommendations
20.1 Dynamic Scheduling
The predicted shipping time can be used to create more realistic delivery schedules.
Instead of using the same fixed scheduling assumptions for all shipments, predicted delivery duration can be incorporated into planning.

20.2 Risk-Based Shipment Prioritization
Shipments with high predicted schedule deviations can be flagged for early intervention.
A simple operational framework is:
Low Risk
   ↓
Normal Monitoring

Medium Risk
   ↓
Additional Monitoring

High Risk
   ↓
Priority Intervention
This allows the organization to focus resources where they are most needed.

20.3 Shipping-Mode Optimization
Shipping-mode performance should be reviewed regularly.
Second Class shipments show the largest predicted schedule gap and should be investigated to determine whether:
•	Scheduling assumptions are too aggressive.
•	Additional capacity is required.
•	Alternative shipping modes should be considered.
•	Dispatch planning should be improved.

20.4 Regional Resource Allocation
Regions with consistently high predicted delay risk can receive additional operational resources.
Possible actions include:
•	Additional warehouse staffing
•	Additional carrier capacity
•	Inventory repositioning
•	Improved dispatch planning
•	Additional monitoring
•	Regional fulfillment capacity
Resources should be targeted rather than distributed equally across all regions.

20.5 Route Planning Improvements
The current dataset does not contain sufficient information for direct route optimization.
Future route-planning models should include:
•	Distance
•	GPS coordinates
•	Traffic conditions
•	Road-network information
•	Weather conditions
•	Vehicle capacity
•	Historical travel time
With these features, route optimization algorithms could identify efficient routes and reduce transportation time.

20.6 Cost Minimization
Optimization should also consider operational cost.
Instead of applying expensive interventions to every shipment, the organization can prioritize high-risk shipments.
A possible strategy is:
High-Risk Shipment
        ↓
Predict Expected Delay
        ↓
Estimate Operational Impact
        ↓
Apply Targeted Intervention
        ↓
Reduce Unnecessary Resource Usage
Actual monetary savings should only be calculated after reliable cost data is available.

20.7 Early-Warning System
The predictive model can be integrated into a logistics dashboard or application.
A possible workflow is:
New Shipment
      ↓
Model Prediction
      ↓
Predicted Shipping Time
      ↓
Compare with Schedule
      ↓
Calculate Delay Risk
      ↓
Alert Operations Team
This can help logistics teams take action before a shipment becomes significantly delayed.


