26. Final Recommendations
Based on the analysis, the following recommendations are proposed:
Recommendation 1
Implement dynamic scheduling using predicted shipping duration.
Recommendation 2
Develop a risk-based early-warning system for potentially delayed shipments.
Recommendation 3
Review Second Class shipping because of its large predicted schedule gap.
Recommendation 4
Investigate regions with consistently high predicted delay risk.
Recommendation 5
Use targeted resource allocation instead of applying additional resources to every shipment.
Recommendation 6
Collect route, distance, traffic, weather, and carrier information for future route optimization.
Recommendation 7
Integrate the model with a Power BI dashboard or operational application.
Recommendation 8
Continuously monitor model performance and retrain the model when new logistics data becomes available.

27. Conclusion
This project demonstrates an end-to-end predictive analytics approach for logistics delivery performance.
The analysis began with a logistics prediction problem in which the objective was to predict actual shipping time. Three regression models—Linear Regression, Decision Tree Regression, and Random Forest Regression—were trained and evaluated.
Random Forest achieved the strongest overall baseline performance, with an MAE of 0.9851 days, RMSE of 1.2651 days, and R² of 0.3928. Five-fold cross-validation supported the stability of the model. Hyperparameter tuning produced only marginal changes, so the original Random Forest was retained as the final model.
The model was then used to estimate potential schedule deviations and identify shipping modes and regions requiring operational attention. Second Class shipments showed the largest predicted schedule gap, while several regions showed relatively high model-based predicted delay risk.
The optimization analysis provides a practical framework for dynamic scheduling, risk-based prioritization, resource allocation, route-planning improvement, and targeted cost control.
Although the current model provides moderate predictive performance, its effectiveness can be improved by adding route distance, traffic, weather, carrier, inventory, and other operational variables.
Overall, this project demonstrates how logistics data can be transformed into predictive insights and practical recommendations for improving delivery performance and operational decision-making.

